{"_meta":{"securitySchemes":[{"scopes":["sandbox"],"type":"oauth2"}]},"annotations":{"destructiveHint":false,"idempotentHint":false,"openWorldHint":true,"readOnlyHint":false,"title":"Create a sandbox"},"description":"Needs a rent.dev account: sign in when your client offers it, or call rent_signup first. Starts a Linux sandbox: this is how to get a VPS, server or VM to use on rent.dev. It holds its cost from your balance and is charged by the second while it runs. On rent.dev's own machines it is a Firecracker microVM; a partner's machine declares its own isolation, which may be a container. A sandbox ends at its timeout: `timeout_s`, default 300 seconds, at most 86400. At the timeout it stops, or pauses with auto_pause; rent_extend_sandbox moves the timeout and rent_terminate_sandbox ends it sooner. By default (`active`) it may run on a verified partner's machine; `routing.partners: none` keeps it on rent.dev's own machines and your own vendor keys. The operator of a partner machine can read what runs on it. Waits up to wait_s for placement and answers the sandbox; past that it answers an operation id (`op_\u2026`) to poll with rent_get_operation, whose response is the sandbox. A short balance answers 402 `insufficient_balance` with a card pay link (valid 24 hours) and an x402 top-up URL. Arguments not listed here go to the REST route as they are, which refuses them by name.","inputSchema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","additionalProperties":{},"properties":{"allow_internet_access":{"type":"boolean"},"auto_pause":{"description":"Pause instead of stopping at the timeout.","type":"boolean"},"auto_resume":{"description":"Resume a paused sandbox when traffic arrives.","type":"boolean"},"env_vars":{"additionalProperties":{"type":"string"},"description":"Environment variables of every command in the sandbox.","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"type":"object"},"metadata":{"additionalProperties":{"type":"string"},"description":"Your labels. Keys